00:00:13.579 --> 00:00:15.770 align:start position:0%
viruses come in many shapes and sizes  ranging from simple protein shells  filled with RNA or DNA to membrane  envelope particles that rival cells in  complexity  HIV is one of those complex viruses  surrounded by a membrane and filled with  a diverse collection of viral and  cellular molecule the genome of HIV  which is composed of two strands of RNA  it's packaged inside a distinctive  cone-shaped capsid which protects the  RNA and delivers it to the cells that  HIV infects  before you begin building the HIV capsid  make sure you have the template scissors  and tape to model the genome you could  use two pieces of string each three  point three meters long step 1 cut out  the model leaving the gray tabs intact  step 2 fold along the white line for a  rounder model that is closer to the  shape of the actual capsid try  assembling the model without this step  step3 tape or glue the gray flaps taping  on the inside and outside will make the  model more stable  step four at this point  insert two pieces of string to represent  the two RNA strands each of them should  be three point three meters long step 5  now you can close your model of the HIV  capsid  for more information about the HIV  capsid and the HIV related proteins and  activities visit the RCS BPD b101  website at our CSP org slash pdb 101
 

